<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # There Are So Many Types of Clubs! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Start a Club!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> There Are So Many Types of Clubs!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Develop a notice that clearly conveys information. Interactive: Participate in a written discussion on paper, through apps, online, or in a forum discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short notice to recruit club members that can be shared in any classroom (e.g., "Join our art club! We meet weekly to create projects together."). Can respond to a question in a forum about club preferences within classroom settings by providing meaningful reasons (e.g., "I chose the environmental club because we can help reduce waste by organizing a school-wide recycling program." "I joined the debate club because it helps me improve my critical thinking and public speaking skills."). Can write a brief post describing club goals and activities that can happen in flexible spaces (e.g., "Our social club meets to share ideas and help each other.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various school clubs (e.g., an art club, a debate club, an environmental club) on the board or projector. Students, in pairs, are asked to write down three activities they associate with each club. For instance, for the art club, they might write: "drawing," "painting," "creating projects." This activates prior knowledge and vocabulary related to "activities" and "clubs" in a written format.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple sentence expressing a club preference and a reason using simple present tense and target vocabulary. For example, the teacher writes on the board: "I like the environmental club because we can help the <i>community</i> with clean-up <i>projects</i>." The teacher then highlights key words like "community" and "projects" and models the pronunciation of 'project' /prɒdʒɛkt/, emphasizing the /pr/ sound. The teacher also models how to use an imperative by writing "<b>Set goals</b> for your club!" and emphasizing the stress on "Set." The pronunciation of verbs ending in -ate like "delegate" and "coordinate" will also be modeled, stressing the third syllable from the end.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled sentences and vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I write 'Join our club to plan exciting <i>events</i>,' what kind of word is 'events'?" (Expected answer: A noun, an activity.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say '<b>Organize</b> a meeting,' what am I doing?" (Expected answer: Giving an instruction.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents various short, authentic written materials: two club recruitment notices (e.g., "Join our Debate Club! Improve your critical thinking and public speaking skills. We meet Tuesdays <i>in</i> the library."), and two excerpts from an online forum discussion where students express preferences for clubs (e.g., "I chose the environmental club because we can help reduce waste by organizing a school-wide recycling program. Last year, I joined a sports club, and I made new friends, but now my <i>interests</i> are different."). The genre is a short notice and a forum discussion post, with an informal register appropriate for high school students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Develop a notice that clearly conveys information. Participate in a written discussion on paper, through apps, online, or in a forum discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the notices and forum posts. They then answer gist questions in writing: "What is the main purpose of Notice A?" and "What is the main idea of Student X's forum post?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match the names of the clubs (from the notices/posts) to their primary goal or activity (e.g., "Environmental Club" to "reduce waste and organize recycling").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify and underline all imperative verbs used in the notices and circle examples of simple present and simple past verbs in the forum posts.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Developing written clarity in notices and forum posts, practicing simple present for descriptions and preferences, imperatives for instructions, and simple past for narrating experiences. Integrating vocabulary such as "clubs," "activities," "goals," "members," "requirements," "manage," "plan," "organize," "create," "participate," "community," "event," "structured," "collaborative."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Activity - Club Notice):</b> The teacher provides a partially completed club notice template on a handout. Students fill in the blanks with appropriate vocabulary words (nouns: <i>members, activities, goals, meetings, project, event</i>; verbs: <i>organize, plan, create, engage, participate</i>; adjectives: <i>social, collaborative, goal-oriented</i>) and grammatical structures (simple present, imperatives). For example: "Join our new _______ club! We _______ exciting _______ every week. Our main _______ is to _______ a big _______ for the school _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion - Forum Post Replies):</b> Students are given prompts mirroring forum questions about club preferences. They complete sentences to express their preferences and reasons, incorporating simple present and simple past. For instance: "What type of club would you like to join and why?" Students write: "I prefer the _______ club because we can _______ (activity). Last year, I _______ (verb in simple past) in a _______ club, and it _______ (verb in simple past) me to improve _______." They must include specific vocabulary like "interests," "responsibilities," "team," "supportive."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Review & Feedback):</b> Students exchange their completed sentences from the semi-controlled practice with a partner. Each student reads their partner's written responses and provides feedback by writing one suggestion for improvement (e.g., "Add more details about the club's <i>goals</i>," "Check the verb tense here," "Could you use an imperative for a suggestion?"). This activity focuses on "responding clearly to feedback and suggestions" in a written context.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Start a Club!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Write a Club Recruitment Notice & Forum Post Reply"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In groups of three, students collectively decide on a new club they want to start at school, considering their "interests." Each group "negotiates roles and responsibilities" for planning the club's "structure" and "activities" by writing them down. For example, "Maria will coordinate the 'meetings,' Juan will 'manage' the 'project' schedule, and Sofia will 'delegate' 'responsibilities' to 'members'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Then, the group will perform two writing tasks:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Club Recruitment Notice:</b> They will collaboratively write a short, clear recruitment notice (50-70 words) for their new club. The notice must include: the club's name, its main "goals" and "activities," meeting "requirements" (e.g., "Join us <i>in</i> Room 205 <i>across from</i> the main office every Friday!"), and an imperative call to action (e.g., "Participate and create awesome projects!"). They should use simple present tense to describe the club and its activities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Forum Post Reply:</b> Additionally, they will collaboratively write a short forum post (30-50 words) describing their club's primary "goals" and "activities," explaining why they believe it's a good addition to the school, using phrases like "Our social club meets to share ideas and help each other." They must integrate one sentence using the simple past to describe a related past experience or an imagined positive outcome (e.g., "Last year, I really wanted a club like this.").</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support and feedback, encouraging the use of target vocabulary and grammatical structures, and ensuring the written products clearly convey information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the writing activity, focusing on the group's collaborative process and individual contributions. A peer assessment checklist will also be used where groups exchange their final written notices and forum posts and evaluate them against specific criteria.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their written Club Recruitment Notice and Forum Post Reply. The teacher collects and reviews them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric for Written Product):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Information Conveyed:</b> Is the club's purpose, activities, and meeting details clear? (2 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least 5-7 target vocabulary words from the list correctly used? (2 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of simple present, imperatives, and simple past. (2 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Completeness of Task:</b> Both notice and forum post are written and meet length requirements. (1 point)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reasoning:</b> Does the forum post provide meaningful reasons for club preferences/goals? (1 point)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short written reflection before leaving.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing the club notice or forum post today, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary word or grammar structure did you feel most confident using in your writing today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and preparation for the next stage of learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did your group 'coordinate' and 'delegate' 'responsibilities' to 'manage' the writing tasks today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can your written communication skills, especially in conveying information clearly, be improved for future tasks?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Most students successfully developed notices and forum posts, demonstrating an understanding of the structure and incorporating target grammar and vocabulary. The peer feedback activity was effective in promoting self-correction. Some groups struggled with integrating the simple past tense naturally into their forum posts; more modeling or a specific scaffolded activity for this might be needed. Additionally, ensuring all members contributed equally to the written output was challenging in some groups. For next lesson, I will ensure explicit roles are assigned for the collaborative writing portions to promote balanced participation. I also need to provide additional examples of narrating past experiences for the forum posts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on the club creation process. Students will summarize and present their club notices and forum posts in a simulated school assembly or online platform. They will need to 'mediate' the information from their written products (notices and posts) into an oral presentation or a digital display, requiring them to 'support' and 'facilitate' information transfer to different audiences, integrating their 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>